# Container development

Requires Docker with Compose v2. Start from the project root:

```sh
docker compose up --build
```

The app uses Go 1.26 and Air v1.67.4. Source files are mounted into the container;
Air polls for changes, regenerates DI wiring and rebuilds the app. Generated
`pfw_gen.go` files and build outputs are excluded to prevent reload loops.
Go dependencies, build cache and binaries have separate persistent volumes.

## Select the application environment

The initial environment is `{{.Environment}}`. Container development does not
force a particular application environment. To change it, copy the root
`.env.example` to `.env`, edit `PFW_ENV`, then recreate the service:

```sh
cp .env.example .env
# Edit PFW_ENV in .env.
docker compose up --build
```

The root `.env` configures Compose. Application configuration files remain in
`env/` for the API. Compose passes the selected environment into both generation
and runtime, so staging-specific DI bindings work during container development.
{{if eq .Template "api"}}
## Development database and API

PostgreSQL is available as `db:5432` inside Compose and `localhost:5432` on the
host. The development database/user/password are `palma`. HTTP defaults to
`http://localhost:8080`; `DEV_HTTP_PORT` and `DEV_DB_PORT` change host ports.

- `local` keeps the API's in-memory storage; PostgreSQL is available but unused.
- `staging` and `prod` use the Compose PostgreSQL database. Pending migrations
  are applied before each app build/start, including SQL changes.

Compose overrides the listen address and SQL connection settings so environment
files do not bind HTTP to container localhost or connect to external databases.
The API waits for PostgreSQL's healthcheck. These credentials and services are
for development, regardless of the selected application environment.

```sh
curl http://localhost:8080/health
docker compose exec app go tool pfw migrate status
```

Database data persists in a named volume. `docker compose down` preserves it;
`docker compose down -v` removes the development data and all cache volumes.
{{end}}
## Useful commands

```sh
docker compose logs -f app
docker compose exec app go test ./...
docker compose down
```

If created with `-framework-dir`, Compose also mounts that checkout read-only at
its original absolute path so the generated Go `replace` works in the container.
Share that directory with Docker Desktop if needed. Air watches application
files; restart the app after changing the external framework checkout.

Air is installed only in the development image; it is not an application
module dependency. Rebuild the image after changing Dockerfile tool versions.
